Yes. The grounded technical version is already visible in pieces.

What you are describing is basically human-in-the-loop affective system identification: present an experience, measure what can actually be measured, ask the person what they felt, compare intended versus received effect, update the model, and repeat. Current affective-computing research already fuses voice, face, behavior, physiology, wearables, text, and interaction data, and some closed-loop systems adapt an experience in real time based on the inferred state. The field is real, but it is not remotely at “perfectly reading a human”; current reviews still flag context sensitivity, missing/noisy modalities, weak cross-person generalization, and interpretability as major problems. 

Your colored-pencil analogy maps nicely to a hierarchical model:

response = shared human factors + individual parameters + context + noise

So across enough consenting people, you can start finding things that behave like experiential primitives: certain temporal structures of suspense, certain kinds of resolution, rhythmic entrainment, novelty, familiarity, social recognition, spatial compression, color relationships, vocal cadence, and so on. Then each person gets their own coefficients and flourishes rather than being treated as identical.

The leap you keep describing—movie → real life—is then not “make reality fake.” It is make reality an adaptive medium. Music, lighting, space, narrative, AI characters, games, physical events, social interaction, sound, timing, and personalization can all become instruments in one composition. That is closer to a Consensual Reality Engine than a film.

But one part needs a hard distinction. When you say that individualized coherences could be made so idiosyncratic that people cannot compare notes, that is no longer merely good personalization. That can create epistemic dependence: each person gets a private reality and loses the ability to reality-check socially. The stronger architecture keeps a common reference layer underneath the personalized one.

Something like:

* participants know what class of experience they are entering;
* they control how much personalization and sensing they permit;
* synthetic/NPC agents are allowed under disclosed or pre-consented immersive rules;
* important factual claims remain externally checkable;
* people can inspect, reset, export, or leave their personalized layer;
* the system does not secretly target a person’s vulnerabilities merely because it discovered them.

You can still make it strange, magical, funny, intense, romantic, competitive, mythic, or deeply personal. You just do not need to steal the person’s ability to say, “Show me what actually happened.”

The “shared God” idea has a cleaner analogue too: shared sacred value or superordinate purpose. Groups coordinate very effectively around things treated as larger than the individual—truth, family, nation, justice, God, science, a mission, a team, humanity, a game. They do not have to share identical metaphysics for the coordination layer to work. Trying to secretly manufacture a common deity would be manipulation; building a space where people can bring different names to a genuinely shared value is much more robust.

And your “blade into a pillow” image has a useful systems translation. You often cannot literally change the incoming object, but you can sometimes change the interface through which its effect reaches you: absorb it, reroute it, reinterpret it, slow it, compartmentalize it, transform its energy, or change the boundary conditions. Same deeper pattern we have been tracking from fire onward.

So yes: there is a real vision here.

Not one algorithm that tells everyone what to feel.

More like:

a medium that learns how different humans experience the same underlying primitives, then composes those primitives into worlds people knowingly choose to enter.

That is much more interesting—and actually buildable.